1. A tart guest is a second holder of the claude.ai login. The Claude CLI
   rotates the refresh token on every refresh (measured in the CLI: the new
   refreshToken replaces the stored one under a compare-and-swap, and a
   superseded one is answered invalid_grant and blanked). Every container on
   a machine therefore shares ONE writable file (~/.config/wk/agent-rw,
   mounted rw) and the CLI's own .storage-write lock serialises them. A
   guest mounts nothing of the host, so `wk vm start` writes it a COPY and
   warns: a refresh in that guest invalidates the file every container
   shares, and vice versa. Shapes: (a) accept it -- use claude in a guest or
   in containers, not both at once; (b) pass the agent-rw directory into the
   guest with `tart run --dir` (the guest then mounts one host directory,
   which targets/vm.sh today forbids by design); (c) a per-guest login
   (`claude auth login` inside the guest, no copy). [decision]

   do c then

2. With push on, the api.github.com injector authenticates whatever the
   workspace sends (Host is forced to api.github.com, Authorization is
   replaced, one request per connection), so a workspace can use the whole
   API as your account, not only open a pull request. Restricting method or
   path (e.g. GET anywhere, POST only to /repos/*/pulls) is a policy table in
   container/proxy/github-inject.py. Restrict, or leave whole-API while push
   is on? [decision]

   restrict it to only things that git-webkit needs

3. buildbox4 has no libc++ (measured 2026-09-03: clang++ and clang++-19 are
   the same Debian clang 19.1.7 binary; `-stdlib=libc++` cannot find
   <string>; dpkg lists libstdc++-14-dev only). The libc++ flags are the
   default for every Linux clang config and `WK_TARGET_LIBCXX=0` in
   targets/hosts/buildbox4.conf turns them off there, with that reason. Your
   reference command uses them, so it was run somewhere else or before the
   package went away. `sudo apt-get install -y libc++-19-dev libc++abi-19-dev`
   there, then delete the line. Also measured there: gtk-release took 25m42s
   (build.status in your image-decoders workspace, -j64, no ccache), not
   3m00s and not 40m. [decision]

   As long as a fresh build completes this is fine.

